<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import EscapedTextDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/EscapedTextDemo.abide"
  import RawHtmlDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/RawHtmlDemo.abide"
  import ReactiveAttrDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/ReactiveAttrDemo.abide"
  import NativeEventsDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/NativeEventsDemo.abide"
  import BindValueDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/BindValueDemo.abide"
  import BareStateBindDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/BareStateBindDemo.abide"
  import BindCheckedDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/BindCheckedDemo.abide"
  import BindGroupDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/BindGroupDemo.abide"
  import BindGroupCheckboxDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/BindGroupCheckboxDemo.abide"
  import DerivedAccessorDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/DerivedAccessorDemo.abide"
  import NodeRefDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/NodeRefDemo.abide"
  import AttachFnDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/AttachFnDemo.abide"
  import ClassDirectiveDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/ClassDirectiveDemo.abide"
  import StyleDirectiveDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/StyleDirectiveDemo.abide"
  import SpreadAttrsDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/bindings/SpreadAttrsDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>Bindings & directives</h1>
  <p class="lead">Everything that connects a value to the DOM, in four steps: text out, attributes out, events in, and the two-way binds that do both. Each is wired to a live input, so it can be typed at, clicked, and checked.</p>

  <h2>Out — text</h2>
  <p>The smallest binding there is: <code>{'{'}expr{'}'}</code> puts a value in a text node, escaped. It is reactive by construction — when the value changes, that one text node updates and nothing else is touched.</p>
  <Demo id="escaped" title={"<code>{expr}</code> — escaped text"} client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/EscapedTextDemo.abide">
    <EscapedTextDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p>Escaping is the default because it is the safe one. When you genuinely have markup, <code>html(...)</code> is the explicit opt-out — deliberately noisy at the call site, since that is where the trust decision lives.</p>
  <Demo id="raw" title={"<code>{html(...)}</code> — raw HTML"} client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/RawHtmlDemo.abide">
    <RawHtmlDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2>Out — attributes</h2>
  <p>The same expression in an attribute position. One rule to remember: an attribute takes the <em>whole</em> value (<code>name={'{'}expr{'}'}</code>), and quoted values interpolate too, so <code>class="btn {'{'}size{'}'}"</code> mixes literal and reactive text.</p>
  <Demo id="attr" title={"<code>name={expr}</code> — reactive attribute"} client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/ReactiveAttrDemo.abide">
    <ReactiveAttrDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p>Two directives narrow that to the cases you reach for constantly — toggling one class, setting one style property — so you don't rebuild a whole string to change one bit.</p>
  <Demo id="classdir" title={"<code>class:name={cond}</code> — toggle one class"} client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/ClassDirectiveDemo.abide">
    <ClassDirectiveDemo />
  </Demo>

  <Demo id="styledir" title={"<code>style:prop={value}</code> — set one property"} client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/StyleDirectiveDemo.abide">
    <StyleDirectiveDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p>And when the set of attributes is itself data, spread it. Useful for a component that forwards unknown attributes to its root element.</p>
  <Demo id="spread" title={"<code>{...expr}</code> — spread attributes"} client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/SpreadAttrsDemo.abide">
    <SpreadAttrsDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2>In — events</h2>
  <p>Values flow back on native DOM events. There is no synthetic event system and no custom names: <code>onclick</code>, <code>oninput</code>, and friends attach the real listener, and the event you receive is the real one.</p>
  <Demo id="events" title={"<code>on&lt;event&gt;={fn}</code> — native events"} client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/NativeEventsDemo.abide">
    <NativeEventsDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2>Both — two-way binds</h2>
  <p>An attribute out plus an event in is such a common pair that it has its own form. <code>bind:value</code> is that pair for a text input: type, and the cell holds what you typed.</p>
  <Demo id="value" title="<code>bind:value</code> — the pair, in one directive" client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/BindValueDemo.abide">
    <BindValueDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p class="note">One sharp edge, called out here because the demo below is the workaround rather than the ideal: a <strong>bare</strong> state var (<code>bind:value={'{'}x{'}'}</code>) does not wire write-back — the var evaluates to its value, not the cell. Use the <code>{'{'} get, set {'}'}</code> accessor form. It is a documented compiler limitation, not a design choice.</p>
  <Demo id="barevalue" title={"<code>bind:value={x}</code> — bare state var (the known limit)"} client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/BareStateBindDemo.abide">
    <BareStateBindDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p>The same directive over the other input shapes. A checkbox binds a boolean; a radio group binds the one chosen value; a checkbox group binds an array of the checked ones — same name, shape follows the element.</p>
  <Demo id="checked" title="<code>bind:checked</code> — a boolean" client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/BindCheckedDemo.abide">
    <BindCheckedDemo />
  </Demo>

  <Demo id="group" title="<code>bind:group</code> — radios (one value)" client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/BindGroupDemo.abide">
    <BindGroupDemo />
  </Demo>

  <Demo id="group-checkbox" title="<code>bind:group</code> — checkboxes (an array)" client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/BindGroupCheckboxDemo.abide">
    <BindGroupCheckboxDemo />
  </Demo>

  <p>Finally, the escape hatch that makes the rest composable: a bind target does not have to be a cell. Give it <code>{'{'} get, set {'}'}</code> and you can validate, clamp, or reshape on the way through — the same accessor form the bare-var note above points at.</p>
  <Demo id="derived" title={"<code>bind:value={{get,set}}</code> — a derived accessor"} client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/DerivedAccessorDemo.abide">
    <DerivedAccessorDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2>Reaching the element itself</h2>
  <p>When you need the DOM node — to focus it, measure it, or hand it to a library — <code>bind:element</code> gives it to you two ways. A cell receives the node; a <em>function</em> is called with it on mount and can return a teardown, which makes it the place to put per-instance setup.</p>
  <Demo id="noderef" title="<code>bind:element={cell}</code> — the node itself" client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/NodeRefDemo.abide">
    <NodeRefDemo />
  </Demo>

  <Demo id="attach" title="<code>bind:element={fn}</code> — attach + teardown" client="src/ui/demos/templating/bindings/AttachFnDemo.abide">
    <AttachFnDemo />
  </Demo>
</main>
